---
description: TypeScript code style and commenting conventions for backend and shared code
globs: [
  "packages/ai-edu-backend/**/*.ts",
  "shared/**/*.ts"
]
alwaysApply: true
---
- Dont write comments if function/method name and params already reflect the intent.
- Code should be lean and easy to read; avoid noisy or obvious comments.
- Prefer self-documenting code with descriptive variable and function names.
- Only add comments when explaining WHY (business logic, edge cases, non-obvious implementation details).
- Remove redundant comments that restate what code already shows.
- Import organization order: standard library → third-party → shared (`@shared/*`) → local (relative paths).
- Application-specific logic should be in `./packages/<app_name>`.
- Base/repeated logic should be in `./shared/<lib_name>`.
- Shared logic should have smart defaults so it is easy to reuse and extend.
- Prefer reusing libraries from `./shared` rather than duplicating logic; if a shared lib needs changes, improve it to be more convenient to use.
- Care about security issues (auth, authorization, input validation, secret handling).
- When a feature relates to backend routing, middleware, or plugins, use context7 to view framework docs before inventing new patterns.
- Errors are handled globally; do not add try/catch unless you need specific handling.
- Only use `type` for type declarations; prefer generics and type inference to reduce duplication.
- Keep code well organized and reusable; follow the patterns in `docs/res/code-style/guide.md`.
- For backend modules and services, follow the architecture in `docs/res/modules/overview.md`.
